(1492454, 195597, 5792, '<td valign="top">Leading lines or curves generally have two purposes. One is to lead the viewer into the scene. The other is to lead you toward the main subject. It is most effective if they come in from the lower left because that is the natural direction humans visually scan a picture from so are easiest to pick up.<br/> <br/>This image meets the challenge in an exceptionally clever and unique way. Not only does it rivet the viewer''s attention to the train but it adds an element of danger since it appears if the photographer does not move then he/she will be run over.  It is difficult to create images where out-of-focus objects in the foreground work but you have pulled it off perfectly. Great job!</td>', 'Leading lines or curves generally have two purposes. One is to lead the viewer into the scene. The other is to lead you toward the main subject. It is most effective if they come in from the lower left because that is the natural direction humans visually scan a picture from so are easiest to pick up. This image meets the challenge in an exceptionally clever and unique way. Not only does it rivet the viewer''s attention to the train but it adds an element of danger since it appears if the photographer does not move then he/she will be run over.  It is difficult to create images where out-of-focus objects in the foreground work but you have pulled it off perfectly. Great job!', '2005-06-29 17:32:42.000000', NULL, 1),
